{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## AC4 PARTIALLY SATISFIED — five alarms applied to AWS, 2026-09-05\n\nOperator authorised the apply. Executed and verified.\n\n**Applied:** `Apply complete! Resources: 5 added, 1 changed, 0 destroyed.`\n\n| Alarm | TreatMissingData | SNS wired |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| rebar-docker-storage-cap-high | breaching | alarm_actions + ok_actions |\n| rebar-docker-buildkit-cache-high | breaching | alarm_actions + ok_actions |\n| rebar-docker-unaccounted-bytes | breaching | alarm_actions + ok_actions |\n| rebar-journal-usage-high | breaching | alarm_actions + ok_actions |\n| rebar-journal-cap-not-in-effect | breaching | alarm_actions + ok_actions |\n\nPlus a description-only update to `rebar-root-disk-pressure`. Zero deletes, zero replaces; the\nonly acting resource type was `aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm`.\n\nAll five read INSUFFICIENT_DATA immediately post-apply purely because they were seconds old; with\n`period=300`, `evaluation_periods=3`, `datapoints_to_alarm=2` and `breaching` they resolve\nwithin ~10-15 minutes. **Expect `rebar-journal-cap-not-in-effect` to go to ALARM and STAY there**\nuntil the `SystemMaxUse=` drop-in reaches the box — see AC5-adjacent note below. That is the alarm\nworking, not a fault.\n\n**The instance never cycled** — `LaunchTime` 2026-09-04T22:07:24Z identical before and after,\nstate `running`. Gerrit still serving: `/config/server/version` 200 (\"3.14.1\"),\n`/mcp` 401 (the correct healthy code, proving the whole TLS -> nginx -> upstream -> app path).\n\n**The `aws_instance.gerrit` user_data drift is GONE.** `user_data.sh` is byte-identical between\nthe stale checkout and `origin/main`, and `main.tf`'s lifecycle block ignores only `ami` (NOT\nuser_data), so it is not being masked. The recovery stop/start earlier tonight applied it.\n\n**Remaining pending Terraform drift: NONE.** A re-plan against `origin/main` returns \"No changes\".\n\n## A hazard surfaced during this apply, now tracked separately\n\nThe main checkout at /Users/joeoakhart/rebar was **14 commits behind origin/main**, so\n`terraform plan` from there returned \"No changes\" — 91 resource_changes, all no-op — for the very\nalarms that did not exist in AWS. Applying that would have been a silent no-op reported as success.\nThe apply was instead run from a throwaway worktree at `origin/main` against the same remote state.\n\nFiled as **jaded-pugnacious-isopod (eebc-6aa1-e45b-4325)**. It also notes the more dangerous\nreverse: an apply from a tree predating a resource DELETION would propose recreating it.\n\n## What remains on this ticket\n\n- **AC1/AC2** — the gate-scratch volume exists and is attached, but is RAW: unformatted, unmounted,\n  no markers. `gate_scratch_mounted` still publishes 0. The validated mount runbook exists and has\n  not been run; that is a separate operator decision.\n- **AC3** — `/var/gerrit` mount still not directly confirmed. Best evidence remains EBS I/O\n  discrimination (49,834 reads vs 49 on the known-raw volume). A read-only on-box\n  `findmnt /var/gerrit` would settle it.\n- **AC5** — the journald `SystemMaxUse=` drop-in is NOT installed. Its install call is in\n  `compose-up.sh`, which autodeploy treats as DETECT-ONLY, and `journald-cap.sh` is in no trigger\n  path at all, so it arrives only on next boot or an explicit `compose-up.sh` run. Tracked also as\n  octahedral-auburn-sunfish (5246-2ec0-1fa6-4637).\n- **AC6** — the declared-vs-provisioned check is not built. This apply is exactly the evidence for\n  why it is needed: nothing but a manual AWS query distinguished \"declared\" from \"live\"."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788569619506057001,"uuid":"0e6ec7c7-ad79-4dc4-ab47-a1c2ad3fd36b"}